  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Which of the following is the phase line of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{3}-4y?$",
  "worked_solution": "To draw the phase line of the differential equation, we proceed using the following three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation. \n\nFirst, we factor the function $f(y),$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3}-4y & =0 \\\\ y(y-2)(y+2) & =0 \\\\ y & =-2,0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-2,$ $y=0$ and $y=2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the intervals of $y$ for which $f(y)>0$ and $f(y)<0.$ \n\nIn this case, we have \n    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3}-4y & >0 \\\\ y(y-2)(y+2) & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nConstructing a sign table, we get the following:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-2,0)\\cup (2,\\infty ),$ and negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (0,2).$\n\n**Step 3**: Draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y)>0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/5fa18a0300f34e5a471dac55a30a1edc838717ef154f3f776f6fabe08536a3e6.png)",
